<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/css/infographic-automation-lane-map.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Archivo:wght@600;700&family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&display=swap">
<div class="n3l-automation-map">
<figure class="fig">

  <header class="mast">
    <span class="eyebrow brand-eyebrow">NEXT3LABS &mdash; small business operations</span>
    <h1>Which job do you <span class="hi">automate</span> first?</h1>
    <p class="sub">6 jobs a 5&ndash;30 person business runs every week. 3 lanes. Blast radius decides the order.</p>
    <div class="mast-rule"></div>
  </header>

  <div class="lanes-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">The sequencing map</span>
    <span class="legend"><i class="chip chip--go" aria-hidden="true"></i> hand over now
      &nbsp;<i class="chip chip--mid" aria-hidden="true"></i> behind a checkpoint
      &nbsp;<i class="chip chip--stop" aria-hidden="true"></i> not yet</span>
  </div>

  <ol class="lanes">

    <li class="lane lane--go">
      <div class="lane-head">
        <span class="lane-tag">Lane 1</span>
        <h2>Hand over now</h2>
        <p class="lane-why">High volume, rules already written, an error costs an apology.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="jobs">
        <li class="job"><h3>Quotes &amp; invoices</h3><p>Line items, agreed rates, GST treatment, InvoiceNow fields &mdash; assembly work with defined rules.</p></li>
        <li class="job"><h3>Customer follow-ups</h3><p>The nine-day-old quote, the revised spec, the service due &mdash; drafted from the thread, sent by a person.</p></li>
        <li class="job"><h3>Report first drafts</h3><p>Weekly sales, monthly stock, the Friday job report. Software assembles; a person reads.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li class="lane lane--mid">
      <div class="lane-head">
        <span class="lane-tag">Lane 2</span>
        <h2>Behind a human checkpoint</h2>
        <p class="lane-why">Useful drafts, real consequences &mdash; someone named approves every time.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="jobs">
        <li class="job"><h3>Rostering &amp; scheduling</h3><p>Software proposes the shifts; the manager who knows the team approves them.</p></li>
        <li class="job"><h3>Inventory reorders</h3><p>A suggested order list a person confirms before it reaches the supplier.</p></li>
        <li class="job"><h3>Payroll &amp; statutory prep</h3><p>CPF, IRAS, MOM paperwork assembled and diff-checked &mdash; a person presses submit.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li class="lane lane--stop">
      <div class="lane-head">
        <span class="lane-tag">Lane 3</span>
        <h2>Not yet</h2>
        <p class="lane-why">The error you cannot apologise your way out of.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="jobs">
        <li class="job"><h3>Unsupervised customer replies</h3><p>Fine on the easy ones; the complaint is the one that costs you the customer.</p></li>
        <li class="job"><h3>Judgement with a commitment</h3><p>Unusual pricing, credit terms, goodwill refunds. The exceptions are the expensive part.</p></li>
        <li class="job"><h3>Final filings</h3><p>A checking layer over submissions, yes. The submission itself stays with a person.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </li>

  </ol>

  <section class="rules">
    <span class="eyebrow rules-label">The one rule</span>
    <ul class="rule-list">
      <li class="rule-item">Sort by the cost of the mistake, not the annoyance of the task</li>
      <li class="rule-item">If nobody is named to verify, the job is not ready</li>
      <li class="rule-item">Jobs move up a lane on track record, not impatience</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <figcaption class="foot">
    <span class="verdict"><b class="num">4</b> questions</span>
    <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
    <span class="verdict"><b class="num">3</b> lanes</span>
    <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
    <span class="verdict twist">the order is the work</span>
  </figcaption>

</figure>
</div>
